WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.450
PETE: So, what about scalability in your architecture?

00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:07.560
How do you handle that and what are some considerations
that you're looking at in terms of making

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:10.650
sure your application architecture is set
that you can scale, because you guys are,

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:12.300
you're not huge right now, right?

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:17.420
But obviously the goal is to processing, I'm
sure hundreds of thousands are more accost

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:20.000
than you are and you keep scaling that up.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.880
VIDA: Right, So, it's also naturally partitioned
right now, where our Simple DB store wanted

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:26.880
to be global.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:32.710
So, we always want all of our users to be
on just one database, to be readily available

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:33.710
to us.

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:41.079
On the other hand, since we're set up with
retailers, we could actually partition, every

00:00:41.079 --> 00:00:44.989
retailer's data can actually be completely
separated and partitioned from the other.

00:00:44.989 --> 00:00:50.980
So, we can serve traffic for specific clients
on specific servers with a different database.

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:57.789
So, I'm never worried about one particular
database having to handle a humungous load.

00:00:57.789 --> 00:01:09.340
For our consumer data, I didn't build simple
DB so I don't know how it works but there's

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:14.660
always things you can do for a consumer, you
could separate our their data and modulo it

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:20.760
according to certain keys, put all your users
at that end with this number on this particular

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:21.760
user data.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:24.060
So, there's still ways to partition the user
data.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:29.880
My guess is that Simple DB probably already
does that and kind of hides that from the

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:34.580
developer without having to figure that out
by serving it automatically on separate servers

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:39.680
without you noticing and labeling the whole
thing as just one big service.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:43.430
PETE: So, you anticipate pushing Simple DB
as far as you possibly can, you're just going

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:48.150
to keep throwing users at it and if it starts
to cry and scream then you'll reconsider.

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:53.090
But right now, you think that's probably going
to suit your scale.

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:55.020
VIDA: I hope so.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:59.560
I would definitely go with the key value store
rather than a SQL store.

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:04.810
I think that SQL has a lot of overhead because
you need to process queries, where you need

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:08.180
to query across it and build indexes across
it and all the other stuff.

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:12.960
And if you want scalability just being able
to store data in different places and partition

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:14.070
it out makes a lot of sense.

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:20.970
I mean at Google we didn't use SQL at all
in our back end servers anyways for speech

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:24.870
recognition or even in our web server.

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:32.450
So, just not really used to thinking of SQL
as the automatic database choice as I think

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:34.200
most of the industry does.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:41.280
PETE: What about the breath of social data
that you have at your disposal from, say,

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:48.230
a Facebook API, one of the challenges there
is that, that data is always changing, right?

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:54.010
And Facebook is basically changing the schema
behind their API and adding fields and deleting

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:58.240
fields and probably merging stuff and whatnot,
they're known to do that right?

00:02:58.240 --> 00:02:59.240
VIDA: Right.

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:01.740
PETE: Does that give you a lot of headaches
and problems?

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:06.770
VIDA: No, because we didn't choose to store
the social data in a SQL store.

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:12.660
But it is true, I think you wanted to have,
we could originally define a user profile

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:16.980
and assume that their DB certain fields, we'd
always have to be updating schema and things

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:17.980
like that.

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:23.470
So, Simple DB is really nice because it doesn't
assume a strict user schema.

00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:29.030
You can still label parts of it but you don't
have to strictly label it that way and predefine

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:30.030
it.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:31.030
PETE: Okay.

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:32.430
VIDA: So, that allows us to be really flexible.

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:36.100
PETE: So, those are really great compliment
to each other.

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:42.810
The social data you're getting from a “not
always what you'd expect” Facebook API and

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:44.620
the schema-less format of Simple DB.

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:45.010
VIDA: Yeah.

